contract on one name recipient. And the venue's note says attention is on how often grants come and

00:04:53.774 --> 00:04:59.274
when they fall relative to the resolution date. Mentioned markets pay if a chosen word is spoken

00:04:59.274 --> 00:05:04.514
at a future public event. And how it works in a new paper is that a language model can be handed

00:05:04.514 --> 00:05:10.134
the live contract price as a starting point. The study is forecasting future language, context

00:05:10.134 --> 00:05:15.694
design for mention markets, and the test bed is earnings calls. Will this company say this

00:05:15.694 --> 00:05:22.474
keyword on the next call? Researchers change two inputs. One was the text news, older call transcripts

00:05:22.474 --> 00:05:28.194
or both. The other was what the model was allowed to do with the contract's probability. Their method,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:33.774
market condition prompting, tells the model to revise that probability with the text instead of

00:05:33.774 --> 00:05:39.654
tossing the price and guessing a fresh base rate. Picture one keyword contract. It already implies

00:05:39.654 --> 00:05:45.194
a chance the word gets said. Feed the model old transcripts and news. Under that prompting,

00:05:45.274 --> 00:05:51.174
it edits the market's number. A blend they call MixMCP then pulls the market probability back in,

00:05:51.594 --> 00:05:57.574
so the model's revision cannot run away from the prior. Three results follow. More context steadily

00:05:57.574 --> 00:06:02.794
lifted forecast quality. Updating the market number, rather than ignoring it, produce better

00:06:02.794 --> 00:06:08.754
calibrated forecasts. The blend beat the market on its own, and holding onto the market prior made

00:06:08.754 --> 00:06:11.934
the results sturdier than the market alone or the model alone.

00:06:12.354 --> 00:06:17.194
The abstract says other recent work has found language models can match human forecasters.

00:06:17.734 --> 00:06:19.834
This paper does not rerun that contest.

00:06:20.414 --> 00:06:22.374
Its claim is about how you build the prompt.

00:06:22.994 --> 00:06:28.414
A second paper, making alternative data work, puts prediction markets in another job, next

00:06:28.414 --> 00:06:33.614
to card transactions and web traffic, as a feed for forecasting how a firm will perform.

00:06:34.174 --> 00:06:35.534
Two agents split the work.

00:06:35.534 --> 00:06:39.054
Look, one picks the firms where a given data channel is likely to matter.

00:06:39.514 --> 00:06:43.534
The other forecasts revenue with context built for that firm and that channel.

00:06:44.014 --> 00:06:48.654
Across four commercial alternative data channels, putting that extra data in the prompt

00:06:48.654 --> 00:06:53.714
beside other financial information beat using either source by itself, and beat ordinary

00:06:53.714 --> 00:06:54.994
forecasting baselines.

00:06:55.614 --> 00:06:57.074
The papers state their own limits.

00:06:57.694 --> 00:07:02.494
Firm level alternative data often cover a short history, matter for only some targets

00:07:02.494 --> 00:07:08.814
or some firms and show up in mismatched channels. On mentioned markets, the weaker setup was a model

00:07:08.814 --> 00:07:14.154
that discarded the contract price and started the base rate over. The blend exists because an

00:07:14.154 --> 00:07:19.614
undamped model move was less sturdy in their tests. Neither abstract quotes and outside critic.

00:07:20.154 --> 00:07:25.574
After that, a contract price is a starting probability with a job. It is not a verdict and it is

00:07:25.574 --> 00:07:30.814
not a poll. Text may move it. In their tests, the sturdier forecast kept the price in the mix.

